import Foundation

enum UXDesignerQuestionBank {
    static let all: [Question] = [
        Question(id: "1", question: "A product manager hands your team a five-page requirements document for a new claims-intake console but no user access is currently permitted. Which approach should you recommend first to ground the design in real behavior?", options: [("A", "Ask the product manager to serve as a proxy user in a contextual walkthrough of the current claims process"), ("B", "Build wireframes directly from the requirements document and defer validation until UAT"), ("C", "Launch a large-scale quantitative survey to statistically validate the requirements document"), ("D", "Skip discovery and rely on Salesforce out-of-the-box claims management patterns")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "When direct end-user access is blocked, a knowledgeable proxy (PM, SME, or customer success manager) walking through the actual process in context surfaces real behavior and friction that a requirements doc alone cannot reveal. B is incorrect because Designing straight from a requirements doc without any behavioral grounding risks encoding stakeholder assumptions rather than actual user needs. C is incorrect because A large quantitative survey is the wrong tool for early generative discovery; it validates known hypotheses rather than exploring an unknown problem space. D is incorrect because Skipping discovery ignores organization-specific workflow nuances that generic patterns won't capture."),
        Question(id: "2", question: "A design team is exploring an undefined problem space for a new field-service dispatch experience versus validating a specific dispatch UI already in prototype. Which two statements correctly match research type to situation? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "Generative research is well-suited to exploring the undefined problem space"), ("B", "Evaluative research is well-suited to validating the existing prototype"), ("C", "Quantitative surveys should be used for both situations to maintain consistency"), ("D", "A/B testing should replace prototype validation entirely"), ("E", "Card sorting is the only valid method for exploring an undefined problem space")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Generative research explores an open problem space to define what to build, while evaluative research tests a specific proposed solution — matching each research type to the situation it fits. C is incorrect because Quantitative surveys can't explore an undefined space meaningfully and are a weak fit for testing a specific prototype's usability. D is incorrect because A/B testing requires two live deployed variants; it can't fully replace direct prototype validation methods like usability testing. E is incorrect because Card sorting evaluates information architecture specifically; it isn't the only valid method for open-ended problem exploration."),
        Question(id: "3", question: "Stakeholders want statistical confidence that a new opportunity form reduces data-entry time across thousands of reps before committing budget to a redesign. Which research method should the designer prioritize?", options: [("A", "One-on-one contextual inquiry with three reps"), ("B", "A single diary study spanning two weeks"), ("C", "A quantitative time-on-task analytics study across the existing rep population"), ("D", "An unmoderated five-second test of the new form's landing screen")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Statistical confidence at scale requires a quantitative method with sufficient sample size, such as analytics-based time-on-task measurement across the full rep population. A is incorrect because Three interviews produce rich qualitative insight but cannot deliver statistical confidence across thousands of users. B is incorrect because A diary study is qualitative and longitudinal, valuable for behavior patterns but not for statistical validation of time savings. D is incorrect because A five-second test evaluates first impressions of a landing screen, not measured task-time reduction."),
        Question(id: "4", question: "A UX designer is preparing to interview a regional sales director about pain points in the current quoting process. Which two discussion guide practices best avoid leading the participant? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "Ask, \"Don't you find the multi-step approval process frustrating?\""), ("B", "Ask, \"On a scale of 1–5, how much do you like the current quoting tool?\""), ("C", "Ask, \"Would you prefer a one-click quote button?\""), ("D", "Ask, \"Walk me through the last time you generated a quote — what happened at each step?\""), ("E", "Ask, \"What was the most difficult part of that process, and why?\"")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [3, 4], explanation: "Open-ended, behavior-anchored questions that ask participants to recount a specific recent experience, or to elaborate on difficulty and reasoning, surface authentic pain points without suggesting an answer. A is incorrect because This question embeds the interviewer's assumption of frustration directly into the phrasing, biasing the response. B is incorrect because A closed numeric scale collects a data point but forecloses the open-ended detail needed to identify root causes. C is incorrect because This asks the participant to evaluate a specific proposed solution rather than describe their actual experience, priming for a \"yes.\""),
        Question(id: "5", question: "During synthesis, a researcher has 40 raw interview notes and needs to identify the most impactful, recurring problem themes before proposing design direction. Which discovery tool should be used?", options: [("A", "An affinity diagram"), ("B", "A journey map"), ("C", "A stakeholder map"), ("D", "A card sorting session")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Affinity diagramming is purpose-built for clustering raw qualitative data points into recurring themes, which is exactly the synthesis task described. B is incorrect because A journey map sequences a single user's experience over time; it doesn't cluster themes across 40 separate interviews. C is incorrect because A stakeholder map documents who is involved and their influence, not thematic patterns in interview data. D is incorrect because Card sorting is a participatory method for evaluating information architecture, not a synthesis technique for existing interview notes."),
        Question(id: "6", question: "A team must decide whether a new dashboard's navigation labels make sense to users, and they already have a firm list of menu items decided by engineering. Which method is most appropriate?", options: [("A", "Open card sorting"), ("B", "Tree testing"), ("C", "Contextual inquiry"), ("D", "Closed card sorting")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Tree testing validates whether users can find items within an existing, fixed hierarchy, which matches a scenario where the menu items are already decided and only findability needs validation. A is incorrect because Open card sorting is used to generate a new categorization scheme, not validate a predetermined one. C is incorrect because Contextual inquiry observes real work in the field; it doesn't isolate a specific IA/navigation hypothesis. D is incorrect because Closed card sorting checks how users would sort items into predefined categories, but doesn't test navigation findability in a live hierarchy."),
        Question(id: "7", question: "A designer wants to confirm two competing icon-button layouts for a service console but has only three days and a small internal sample. Which validation approach best fits these constraints?", options: [("A", "A statistically powered A/B test rolled out to all agents"), ("B", "A twelve-week longitudinal diary study"), ("C", "A moderated usability test with five to seven internal participants"), ("D", "A formal SUS survey administered company-wide")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "A small moderated usability test with five to seven participants is well suited to catching major usability issues quickly within tight timelines, matching Nielsen's research on diminishing returns beyond that sample size for qualitative testing. A is incorrect because A statistically powered A/B test needs a large sample and longer runtime than three days allows, and requires a live environment to measure behavior at scale. B is incorrect because A twelve-week diary study is a longitudinal method entirely mismatched to a three-day deadline. D is incorrect because A company-wide SUS survey produces a perception score, not the specific comparative usability findings needed to choose between two layouts."),
        Question(id: "8", question: "A UX architect is comparing the new opportunity console against three competitors' CRM interfaces to establish baseline expectations before user research has even begun. Which two statements about this activity are accurate? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "This activity is called competitive analysis"), ("B", "Competitive analysis is valuable as an early input when direct user access is not yet available"), ("C", "Card sorting is a more appropriate label for this activity"), ("D", "This activity requires a live production environment to be valid"), ("E", "Contextual inquiry, not competitive analysis, is the correct term for this activity")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "This benchmarking activity is called competitive analysis; it benchmarks against industry-standard patterns and can be conducted without user access, making it a strong early input to establish baseline design requirements. C is incorrect because Card sorting is a participatory IA method requiring user participants, not a desk-based competitive benchmarking exercise. D is incorrect because Competitive analysis does not require a live production environment; it can be done well before any design exists. E is incorrect because Contextual inquiry requires observing real users in their environment, which is the opposite of this no-user-access scenario."),
        Question(id: "9", question: "Interview notes reveal that field technicians say they \"always check inventory before starting a job,\" but observation shows they frequently skip this step under time pressure. Which discovery tool best captures this say/do gap for the team?", options: [("A", "An empathy map"), ("B", "A stakeholder map"), ("C", "A service blueprint"), ("D", "A validation rule audit")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "The empathy map's Say and Do quadrants are specifically designed to expose contradictions between stated behavior and observed behavior, which is exactly the gap described. B is incorrect because A stakeholder map documents organizational influence and interest, not individual behavioral contradictions. C is incorrect because A service blueprint maps front-stage and back-stage processes across systems, not an individual's say/do discrepancy. D is incorrect because A validation rule audit is a declarative configuration review, unrelated to behavioral research synthesis."),
        Question(id: "10", question: "A team wants to compare completion rates of a redesigned case-submission form against the legacy form once both are already live and stable. Which testing type is most appropriate?", options: [("A", "Formative testing"), ("B", "A/B testing"), ("C", "A heuristic evaluation"), ("D", "A think-aloud protocol")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "A/B testing measures real behavioral outcomes between two live variants at scale, which fits comparing completion rates of two stable, deployed forms. A is incorrect because Formative testing is meant to improve a design mid-development, not compare two already-finished live variants. C is incorrect because A heuristic evaluation is an expert-led inspection against usability principles, not a live behavioral comparison. D is incorrect because A think-aloud protocol captures verbalized reasoning during a session; it doesn't measure aggregate completion-rate differences."),
        Question(id: "11", question: "A Success Architect is gathering requirements for a redesigned partner portal and stakeholders keep describing solutions (\"we need a button here\") instead of problems. Which two techniques help redirect the conversation productively? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "Ask the stakeholder to sketch the button placement immediately"), ("B", "Approve the button request to keep the meeting moving"), ("C", "Probe with \"why\" questions to uncover the underlying goal behind the requested solution"), ("D", "Escalate to a developer to scope the button's technical feasibility"), ("E", "Ask what problem the stakeholder is trying to solve if the button didn't exist")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [2, 4], explanation: "Asking \"why\" and reframing around the underlying problem both surface the real user goal or pain point behind a premature solution request, which is essential before committing to any specific design. A is incorrect because Sketching the requested solution immediately skips understanding whether it actually solves the right problem. B is incorrect because Approving the request without understanding intent risks building the wrong solution entirely. D is incorrect because Technical feasibility is irrelevant before the underlying problem is even understood."),
        Question(id: "12", question: "Which pairing correctly matches a discovery tool to its primary use given limited stakeholder time for a Salesforce console redesign kickoff?", options: [("A", "Affinity diagram — to identify who has influence and interest in the project"), ("B", "Journey map — to identify who has influence and interest in the project"), ("C", "Empathy map — to identify who has influence and interest in the project"), ("D", "Stakeholder map — to identify who has influence and interest in the project")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "A stakeholder map is purpose-built to plot influence and interest across people connected to a project, which directly supports kickoff planning under time constraints. A is incorrect because An affinity diagram clusters qualitative research themes, unrelated to mapping stakeholder influence. B is incorrect because A journey map sequences a user's experience over time, not organizational influence and interest. C is incorrect because An empathy map captures an individual user's thoughts, feelings, and behaviors, not stakeholder influence."),
        Question(id: "13", question: "During a heuristic evaluation of an existing Salesforce case console, a designer notices the system gives no indication after a user clicks \"Save\" whether the action succeeded. Which Nielsen heuristic is violated?", options: [("A", "Visibility of system status"), ("B", "Consistency and standards"), ("C", "Error prevention"), ("D", "Recognition rather than recall")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Visibility of system status requires the interface to keep users informed through appropriate feedback within reasonable time, which is exactly what's missing after the Save action. B is incorrect because Consistency and standards concerns whether elements behave the same way across the interface, not feedback timing on a single action. C is incorrect because Error prevention is about stopping mistakes before they happen, not confirming after a successful action. D is incorrect because Recognition rather than recall concerns minimizing memory load through visible options, unrelated to post-save feedback."),
        Question(id: "14", question: "A team has quantitative analytics showing a 40% drop-off on step 3 of a five-step onboarding flow but no insight into why. Which two next steps are appropriate? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "Redesign step 3 immediately based on the drop-off number alone"), ("B", "Conduct qualitative research such as a moderated usability test to understand the cause of the drop-off"), ("C", "Remove step 3 entirely without further investigation"), ("D", "Review session recordings or heatmaps for step 3 to look for behavioral clues before committing to a fix"), ("E", "Increase the sample size of the same analytics report to get more precision on the drop-off rate")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 3], explanation: "Analytics reveal that a problem exists but not why, so qualitative research and behavioral evidence like session recordings are both needed to understand the underlying cause before committing to a redesign. A is incorrect because Redesigning without understanding the cause risks fixing the wrong thing. C is incorrect because Removing the step without diagnosis could eliminate something users actually need, just poorly presented. E is incorrect because More precision on the same metric still doesn't explain the \"why\" behind the behavior."),
        Question(id: "15", question: "A researcher wants to present findings from six user interviews to skeptical executive stakeholders who have never seen a UX research readout. Which approach builds the most organizational support?", options: [("A", "Present a bulleted list of recommended features with no supporting evidence"), ("B", "Present only the researcher's personal opinion of what should change"), ("C", "Present direct user quotes, video clips, and observed behaviors tied to specific business metrics"), ("D", "Skip the readout and proceed directly to prototyping")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Pairing concrete evidence — quotes, observed behavior, and metrics — builds credibility and organizational buy-in far more effectively than assertions alone for stakeholders unfamiliar with UX research. A is incorrect because A recommendation list without evidence gives skeptical stakeholders no way to trust the conclusions. B is incorrect because Personal opinion without evidence undermines credibility with an audience that hasn't bought into UX research yet. D is incorrect because Skipping the readout forfeits the opportunity to build stakeholder alignment before design work begins."),
        Question(id: "16", question: "Which two research activities together are most appropriate when a team must both explore an undefined onboarding problem and later validate their chosen solution before launch? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "Contextual inquiry during the exploration phase"), ("B", "A/B testing during the exploration phase"), ("C", "Usability testing of a prototype during the validation phase"), ("D", "A stakeholder map during the validation phase"), ("E", "An affinity diagram during the validation phase")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 2], explanation: "Contextual inquiry is a generative method well-suited to exploring an undefined problem space, while usability testing of a prototype is the standard evaluative method for validating a proposed solution before launch. B is incorrect because A/B testing requires a live, deployed variant to compare, which doesn't exist during early exploration of an undefined problem. D is incorrect because A stakeholder map identifies influence and interest for planning, not solution validation. E is incorrect because An affinity diagram synthesizes raw research data into themes; it isn't a validation activity for a chosen solution."),
        Question(id: "17", question: "A team is deciding what artifact to hand off to engineers before development begins on a new console redesign. Which UX deliverable is most appropriate at this stage?", options: [("A", "A single low-fidelity sketch with no annotations"), ("B", "A rough empathy map created during early discovery interviews"), ("C", "A stakeholder map listing project sponsors"), ("D", "A high-fidelity interactive prototype validated with users, annotated with interaction specs")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Engineering handoff requires a validated, detailed artifact that specifies interactions precisely enough to build from, which only a validated high-fidelity prototype provides. A is incorrect because An unannotated low-fidelity sketch lacks the specification detail engineers need to implement correctly. B is incorrect because An empathy map documents user attitudes from discovery, not build-ready interaction detail. C is incorrect because A stakeholder map has no design content for engineers to build against."),
        Question(id: "18", question: "A UX designer must decide between a wireframe and a mockup to review layout structure with a business stakeholder who has no design background. Structure is the priority, visual polish is not yet needed. Which fidelity level fits?", options: [("A", "Wireframe"), ("B", "Mockup"), ("C", "Interactive prototype"), ("D", "Live build")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "A wireframe conveys structure and layout without visual design distractions, matching a review focused purely on structural decisions. B is incorrect because A mockup adds visual design detail that isn't needed yet and could distract from a structural conversation. C is incorrect because An interactive prototype implies functioning interactions, which is more fidelity than a structure-only review requires. D is incorrect because A live build requires actual development investment before the structure has even been agreed upon."),
        Question(id: "19", question: "Which fidelity level should a team use to validate whether users can successfully complete a multi-step checkout flow, including branching logic and transitions?", options: [("A", "Static mockup"), ("B", "Interactive prototype"), ("C", "Wireframe"), ("D", "Empathy map")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Validating flow completion with branching logic requires users to actually interact with transitions, which only an interactive prototype supports. A is incorrect because A static mockup shows visual design on one screen but can't demonstrate branching or transitions. C is incorrect because A wireframe lacks the interactivity needed to test flow completion. D is incorrect because An empathy map is a research synthesis tool, not a testable design artifact."),
        Question(id: "20", question: "A stakeholder insists on skipping wireframes and jumping straight to a polished mockup to \"save time.\" Which two risks should the designer raise? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "Mockups cannot be created faster than wireframes under any circumstance"), ("B", "Mockups are not compatible with Lightning Experience"), ("C", "Visual polish may cause stakeholders to focus on colors and fonts instead of catching structural problems early"), ("D", "Wireframes are required by Salesforce governance policy"), ("E", "Structural issues found late, after visual polish is applied, are more costly to fix")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [2, 4], explanation: "High visual fidelity tends to anchor feedback on surface-level polish rather than structure, and structural problems caught late — after time was spent on visual polish — are more expensive to fix than if caught at the wireframe stage. A is incorrect because Speed of creation isn't the core risk being raised; the risk is the quality of feedback received. B is incorrect because Mockups are a design fidelity level, not something Salesforce technically restricts. D is incorrect because There is no such governance requirement; this misstates the actual concern."),
        Question(id: "21", question: "A designer is building a future-state journey map for a redesigned lead qualification process. What is the primary purpose of this artifact compared to an as-is journey map?", options: [("A", "To document the exact current pain points users experience today"), ("B", "To calculate a System Usability Scale score"), ("C", "To list all stakeholders and their level of influence"), ("D", "To depict the desired end-state experience the design should achieve")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "A future-state journey map illustrates the intended, improved experience the design aims to create, in contrast to an as-is map that documents the present state. A is incorrect because Documenting current pain points is exactly the function of an as-is journey map, not a future-state one. B is incorrect because A SUS score is a usability testing metric, unrelated to journey mapping. C is incorrect because Stakeholder influence belongs in a stakeholder map, not a journey map of either type."),
        Question(id: "22", question: "A back-office claims process involves front-stage agent interactions and back-stage system integrations across three departments. Which artifact best captures both layers together?", options: [("A", "A service blueprint"), ("B", "A basic journey map"), ("C", "An empathy map"), ("D", "A stakeholder map")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "A service blueprint extends the journey map by adding back-stage processes, systems, and departmental handoffs, which is necessary when back-office complexity spans multiple systems. B is incorrect because A basic journey map typically covers only the front-stage user experience, missing back-stage system detail. C is incorrect because An empathy map captures one user's internal state, not cross-department process flow. D is incorrect because A stakeholder map shows influence and interest, not process sequencing across systems."),
        Question(id: "23", question: "A field service dispatcher must approve or reject requests while standing outdoors in bright sunlight using a phone. Which corporate branding risk is most relevant to flag during design review?", options: [("A", "The logo placement violates trademark guidelines"), ("B", "The primary brand color palette may not meet sufficient contrast for outdoor visibility"), ("C", "The font choice is inconsistent with the desktop app"), ("D", "The app icon does not match the corporate style guide")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Low-contrast brand colors that look fine indoors can become unreadable in bright outdoor light, directly affecting a mobile field worker's ability to complete the task. A is incorrect because Trademark placement is a legal/brand compliance concern, not a usability risk tied to outdoor visibility. C is incorrect because Font inconsistency is a branding cohesion issue, not the priority risk for outdoor legibility. D is incorrect because App icon styling has no bearing on in-app contrast or outdoor readability."),
        Question(id: "24", question: "During a Lightning Experience customization project, a developer proposes overriding SLDS color tokens with custom CSS to match a new sub-brand palette. What UX risk should the designer raise?", options: [("A", "Custom CSS always breaks Lightning page load performance"), ("B", "SLDS tokens cannot technically be overridden under any circumstance"), ("C", "Overriding SLDS tokens can create visual inconsistency with native Lightning elements users already trust"), ("D", "Custom CSS is required for all Salesforce rebranding work")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "SLDS tokens exist to keep custom components visually aligned with native Lightning Experience elements, so overriding them risks a jarring, inconsistent look that undermines user trust and learned patterns. A is incorrect because While CSS can affect performance, that's not the specific UX consistency risk being raised here. B is incorrect because SLDS tokens can technically be overridden; the concern is about the consequence, not technical feasibility. D is incorrect because Custom CSS overrides are not a required step for standard rebranding; declarative theming often suffices."),
        Question(id: "25", question: "A designer is evaluating a record detail page and notices related fields are scattered without clear grouping, forcing users to hunt for information. Which principle should guide the redesign?", options: [("A", "Jobs-to-be-done framing — reframe the stakeholder's request"), ("B", "Cognitive load theory — remove all fields from the page"), ("C", "POUR principle of Robust — ensure compatibility with assistive technology"), ("D", "Gestalt principle of proximity — group related fields visually closer together")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "The Gestalt principle of proximity states that elements placed close together are perceived as related, so grouping related fields visually addresses the scattered layout problem directly. A is incorrect because Jobs-to-be-done reframes a feature request into a user goal; it doesn't address a layout arrangement problem. B is incorrect because Removing all fields eliminates needed information rather than addressing the grouping problem. C is incorrect because Robust concerns assistive technology compatibility, not visual grouping of fields."),
        Question(id: "26", question: "A proposed color scheme uses light gray text (contrast ratio 3.2:1) on a white background for standard body copy in a Salesforce record page. Does this meet WCAG 2.1 Level AA?", options: [("A", "No, because normal text requires a minimum contrast ratio of 4.5:1"), ("B", "Yes, because 3.2:1 exceeds the minimum for large text"), ("C", "Yes, because WCAG AA does not apply to Salesforce customizations"), ("D", "No, because all text must be pure black on pure white")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "WCAG 2.1 Level AA requires a minimum 4.5:1 contrast ratio for normal-sized text, and 3.2:1 falls short of that threshold. B is incorrect because 3.2:1 doesn't even meet the lower 3:1 threshold reserved for large text, and this is normal body text anyway. C is incorrect because WCAG guidance applies regardless of platform; Salesforce customizations are not exempt. D is incorrect because WCAG does not require pure black on white — it requires meeting a minimum ratio, which can be achieved many ways."),
        Question(id: "27", question: "A screen reader user reports being unable to determine which form fields are required on a Salesforce case submission form. Which POUR principle is being violated?", options: [("A", "Operable"), ("B", "Perceivable"), ("C", "Understandable"), ("D", "Robust")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Perceivable requires that information (including required-field status) be presented so all users, including those using screen readers, can perceive it — missing required-field cues is a perceivability failure. A is incorrect because Operable concerns the ability to interact with and navigate controls, not whether information is announced. C is incorrect because Understandable concerns predictability and clarity of content/behavior, not whether the cue is exposed to assistive tech at all. D is incorrect because Robust concerns compatibility with a range of technologies broadly, which is a narrower issue than this specific missing cue."),
        Question(id: "28", question: "A keyboard-only user reports getting stuck inside a modal dialog on a custom Lightning page with no way to tab back out. Which POUR principle does this violate?", options: [("A", "Perceivable"), ("B", "Understandable"), ("C", "Operable"), ("D", "Robust")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "A keyboard trap prevents a user from operating the interface using standard input methods, which is a direct violation of the Operable principle. A is incorrect because Perceivable concerns whether content can be sensed at all, not whether it can be exited via keyboard. B is incorrect because Understandable concerns predictability of content and operation, a different concern than being physically unable to exit. D is incorrect because Robust concerns broad compatibility with assistive technologies generally, not this specific keyboard interaction failure."),
        Question(id: "29", question: "A designer wants to reduce the cognitive load of a lengthy new-hire intake form used only once per employee. Which technique best applies progressive disclosure?", options: [("A", "Show all forty fields on one long scrolling page"), ("B", "Auto-fill all fields with default values without user review"), ("C", "Replace the form with a single free-text box"), ("D", "Break the form into a multi-step screen flow revealing only relevant fields per step")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Progressive disclosure reveals only the information relevant to the current step, reducing the mental burden of seeing all forty fields simultaneously. A is incorrect because Showing all fields at once is the opposite of progressive disclosure and increases cognitive load. B is incorrect because Auto-filling without any review risks incorrect data going unnoticed, trading cognitive load for accuracy risk. C is incorrect because A single free-text box removes structure entirely and creates ambiguity about what information is needed."),
        Question(id: "30", question: "After a user clicks \"Submit\" on a case form, the page shows no spinner, no confirmation, and no visual change for several seconds. Which UX principle addresses this gap?", options: [("A", "Affordance and feedback — the system should communicate its status after the action"), ("B", "Gestalt similarity — related elements should share visual styling"), ("C", "Jobs-to-be-done — reframe the button as a job to be accomplished"), ("D", "Information architecture — reorganize the navigation menu")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Feedback principles require the system to visibly acknowledge an action (e.g., a spinner or confirmation) so users understand something is happening, which is missing here. B is incorrect because Gestalt similarity concerns visual grouping via shared styling, unrelated to post-action feedback. C is incorrect because Jobs-to-be-done reframes requirements, not post-submission system feedback. D is incorrect because Information architecture concerns navigation structure, not real-time action feedback."),
        Question(id: "31", question: "New users repeatedly can't find the \"Cases\" tab because it's labeled \"Tickets\" in this org's customized navigation, contrary to what every help article says. Which IA principle is being violated?", options: [("A", "Navigation labels should always match the underlying API name exactly"), ("B", "Users should be able to predict where to find functionality based on consistent, familiar labeling"), ("C", "Labels should be renamed frequently to keep the interface fresh"), ("D", "IA labeling has no measurable effect on user behavior")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Good information architecture relies on predictable, consistent labeling so users can find functionality without needing to be retrained, and diverging from familiar terms like \"Cases\" undermines that. A is incorrect because Labels don't need to match API names exactly; the issue here is inconsistency with documentation and user expectations, not API alignment. C is incorrect because Frequent relabeling would make navigation less predictable, not more usable. D is incorrect because This directly contradicts established UX research on findability and learned navigation patterns."),
        Question(id: "32", question: "A validation error on a Salesforce opportunity form displays only the text \"Error: INVALID\\_FIELD\\_FOR\\_INSERT\\_UPDATE.\" Which practice should the designer recommend to improve this?", options: [("A", "Leave the technical error message as-is since developers understand it"), ("B", "Remove all error messages from the form entirely"), ("C", "Rewrite the message in plain, actionable language telling the user what to fix and how"), ("D", "Translate the message into multiple languages without changing its content")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Actionable, non-technical microcopy tells users specifically what went wrong and how to fix it, which is essential since most end users can't interpret raw system error codes. A is incorrect because Leaving a raw technical code as-is fails end users who have no context for what it means. B is incorrect because Removing error messages entirely leaves users with no guidance when something goes wrong. D is incorrect because Translating a cryptic message keeps it equally unusable in every language; the content itself must change first."),
        Question(id: "33", question: "A Salesforce mobile app user is filling out a work order on their phone while standing at a job site. Which mobile UX constraint is most relevant to prioritizing field visibility on the compact layout?", options: [("A", "Users have unlimited screen real estate on mobile"), ("B", "Offline capability is irrelevant to field UX"), ("C", "Mobile users always have full keyboard access"), ("D", "Touch targets and thumb zones limit how many fields and actions can be comfortably placed on screen")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Small screens, touch-target sizing, and thumb-reach zones constrain how much can be usefully placed on a mobile screen, which is exactly why compact layouts must prioritize only the most critical fields. A is incorrect because Mobile screens have significantly less real estate than desktop, the opposite of this claim. B is incorrect because Offline capability is highly relevant for field workers who may lose connectivity at job sites. C is incorrect because Mobile users typically rely on touch input, not a full physical keyboard."),
        Question(id: "34", question: "A designer must decide whether Lightning Experience's page layout behavior is responsive or adaptive. Which correctly describes the approach Lightning Experience uses?", options: [("A", "Responsive design, where the layout fluidly adjusts to available screen size"), ("B", "Adaptive design, serving entirely distinct fixed layouts for each screen size"), ("C", "Neither approach; Lightning Experience layouts are static and never adjust"), ("D", "Print-first design optimized for paper output")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Lightning Experience and Experience Cloud use responsive design, fluidly adapting component layout to the available screen width rather than serving separate fixed-breakpoint layouts. B is incorrect because Adaptive design serves distinct layouts per breakpoint, which is not how Lightning Experience primarily behaves. C is incorrect because Lightning pages do adjust to screen size; claiming otherwise misrepresents the platform. D is incorrect because Print-first design is unrelated to any Salesforce UI rendering approach."),
        Question(id: "35", question: "Which two considerations should most influence whether a Salesforce mobile compact layout is required versus using the standard desktop layout? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "Whether the primary users frequently access records on small mobile screens in the field"), ("B", "Whether the org uses a custom color theme"), ("C", "Whether the fields shown are the most task-critical ones for quick mobile decisions"), ("D", "Whether the desktop layout uses a two-column arrangement"), ("E", "Whether the report folder is shared publicly")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 2], explanation: "A compact layout is warranted when users primarily work from small mobile screens and need only the most task-critical fields surfaced quickly, since compact layouts exist specifically to solve for constrained space and urgency. B is incorrect because A custom color theme is a branding decision unrelated to whether a compact layout is needed. D is incorrect because The desktop layout's column count doesn't determine mobile compact layout necessity. E is incorrect because Report folder sharing settings have no bearing on record page layout decisions."),
        Question(id: "36", question: "A design team is brainstorming many possible solutions to a complex service-scheduling problem before narrowing down to one direction. Which mode of thinking are they using?", options: [("A", "Convergent thinking"), ("B", "Divergent thinking"), ("C", "Evaluative research"), ("D", "Summative testing")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Divergent thinking generates a wide range of possible ideas without judgment, which matches the open brainstorming phase described before any narrowing occurs. A is incorrect because Convergent thinking narrows options down to a decision, which is the opposite of the brainstorming phase described. C is incorrect because Evaluative research tests a specific existing solution, not idea generation. D is incorrect because Summative testing evaluates a finished design against benchmarks, unrelated to early ideation."),
        Question(id: "37", question: "A client restricts direct access to end users during a project. The design team instead shadows a supervisor performing the actual work and uses think-aloud commentary. Which human-centered design technique is this?", options: [("A", "Card sorting"), ("B", "A/B testing"), ("C", "Contextual inquiry"), ("D", "A stakeholder map")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Contextual inquiry involves observing and questioning someone as they perform real work in their actual environment, which matches shadowing the supervisor with think-aloud commentary. A is incorrect because Card sorting evaluates categorization of items, not observed real-world work. B is incorrect because A/B testing compares two live variants in production, not an observational technique. D is incorrect because A stakeholder map documents organizational influence, not behavioral observation."),
        Question(id: "38", question: "A team has just synthesized research findings into a clear problem statement and is now generating a wide range of potential solution concepts. According to the Double Diamond model, which phase are they in?", options: [("A", "Discover"), ("B", "Define"), ("C", "Deliver"), ("D", "Develop")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "The Develop phase is where teams generate and explore a range of potential solutions after the problem has already been defined, matching this scenario exactly. A is incorrect because Discover is the initial research phase before a problem statement exists, which has already been completed here. B is incorrect because Define is where the problem statement itself is created, which has already happened in this scenario. C is incorrect because Deliver is the final phase focused on testing and shipping a chosen solution, not generating concepts."),
        Question(id: "39", question: "Midway through building a high-fidelity prototype, usability feedback reveals the team misunderstood a core user need identified in early research. What should the team do next?", options: [("A", "Cycle back to an earlier discovery or define activity to correct the misunderstood need before continuing"), ("B", "Continue building the current prototype to avoid wasting sunk effort"), ("C", "Ship the current design and address the issue in a future release"), ("D", "Ignore the feedback since it came from only one usability session")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Human-centered design is iterative, and discovering a fundamental misunderstanding of user needs warrants cycling back to correct the foundation rather than building further on a flawed premise. B is incorrect because Continuing to build on a wrong premise compounds wasted effort rather than avoiding it. C is incorrect because Shipping a design built on a misunderstood need risks a poor launch experience that could have been caught earlier. D is incorrect because Even a single session's feedback about a core misunderstanding warrants investigation, not dismissal, especially if it aligns with earlier signals."),
        Question(id: "40", question: "A stakeholder requests \"a dashboard with more charts.\" Applying jobs-to-be-done framing, which three questions or actions help the designer reframe this request appropriately? (Choose 3)", options: [("A", "\"How many charts specifically do you want?\""), ("B", "\"What decision are you trying to make, and what information do you need to make it confidently?\""), ("C", "\"Which chart library does engineering prefer?\""), ("D", "\"Can we schedule this for the next sprint?\""), ("E", "\"Who looks at this dashboard, and what do they do right after looking at it?\""), ("F", "\"What would happen if this data weren't available at all?\"")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 4, 5], explanation: "Jobs-to-be-done framing asks what underlying goal or job the user is trying to accomplish — the decision being made, who acts on the information, and what happens without it — reframing a feature request into the actual outcome the user needs support for. A is incorrect because Asking for a chart count accepts the solution request at face value instead of uncovering the underlying goal. C is incorrect because Chart library choice is a technical implementation detail, unrelated to understanding the user's job to be done. D is incorrect because Scheduling is a project-management concern, not a reframing of the underlying need."),
        Question(id: "41", question: "A proposed Salesforce solution mirrors the underlying data model (objects, fields, relationships) exactly, but users describe their work in terms of customer relationships and outcomes, not data records. What gap should the designer flag?", options: [("A", "The solution needs a more colorful theme"), ("B", "The solution needs more custom objects to be complete"), ("C", "The solution matches the system's data model but not the user's mental model of their work"), ("D", "The gap is irrelevant since Salesforce is a data-first platform")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "A well-designed solution should match how users conceptually think about their work (their mental model), not just mirror the underlying data structure, and this scenario describes exactly that misalignment. A is incorrect because A color theme change is cosmetic and doesn't address the conceptual mismatch described. B is incorrect because Adding more custom objects would deepen the data-model orientation rather than resolve the mental-model mismatch. D is incorrect because Dismissing the mental model gap ignores a core human-centered design principle regardless of platform."),
        Question(id: "42", question: "A team lists design assumptions on a 2x2 grid by how confident they are and how much risk each carries if wrong. Which technique is this, and what should the team do with high-risk, low-confidence assumptions?", options: [("A", "A SUS questionnaire; average their scores"), ("B", "A stakeholder map; deprioritize high-risk assumptions permanently"), ("C", "A journey map; document them as known pain points"), ("D", "An assumption map; validate high-risk, low-confidence assumptions through research before committing")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "An assumption map plots assumptions by confidence and risk specifically to prioritize which ones need validation first, and high-risk, low-confidence assumptions are exactly the ones that should be tested before the team commits further. A is incorrect because A SUS questionnaire measures perceived usability post-test, unrelated to prioritizing unvalidated assumptions. B is incorrect because A stakeholder map addresses influence and interest, not assumption risk, and permanently deprioritizing risky unknowns ignores them rather than resolving them. C is incorrect because A journey map sequences experience over time; it isn't the tool for categorizing assumption risk."),
        Question(id: "43", question: "With a limited research budget and a two-week timeline, a team wants to involve actual users in shaping a new onboarding flow rather than just testing a finished design. Which approach fits best?", options: [("A", "A lightweight co-design workshop where users sketch and prioritize ideas alongside the design team"), ("B", "A single unmoderated usability test after the design is finalized"), ("C", "A twelve-week ethnographic field study"), ("D", "A stakeholder-only design review with no user involvement")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "A lightweight co-design workshop directly involves users in shaping the design within a short timeframe and limited budget, matching the constraint of wanting participatory input quickly. B is incorrect because An unmoderated test after the design is finalized only evaluates, it doesn't involve users in shaping the design itself. C is incorrect because A twelve-week ethnographic study far exceeds the two-week timeline available. D is incorrect because A stakeholder-only review excludes actual users entirely, contradicting the goal of user involvement."),
        Question(id: "44", question: "A team has one week to go from problem framing to a tested prototype for a new internal tool concept. Which methodology's five phases (Map, Sketch, Decide, Prototype, Test) fits this compressed timeline?", options: [("A", "Full generative discovery research program"), ("B", "Design Sprint methodology"), ("C", "A twelve-week diary study"), ("D", "A formal SUS benchmarking program")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "The Design Sprint methodology is explicitly structured as a compressed, time-boxed process moving from problem framing through a tested prototype in about a week, matching this exact constraint. A is incorrect because A full generative discovery program typically requires weeks to months, incompatible with a one-week timeline. C is incorrect because A twelve-week diary study is a longitudinal method, far exceeding the available time. D is incorrect because SUS benchmarking measures usability perception after a design exists; it isn't a rapid ideation-to-prototype methodology."),
        Question(id: "45", question: "Which three statements correctly describe how inclusive design relates to accessibility as applied to a Salesforce console redesign? (Choose 3)", options: [("A", "Accessibility and inclusive design are identical terms with no meaningful difference"), ("B", "Accessibility only applies to mobile apps, while inclusive design only applies to desktop"), ("C", "Inclusive design is a broader methodology benefiting all users, including but not limited to those with permanent disabilities"), ("D", "Accessibility compliance, such as WCAG, is a narrower technical standard within the broader inclusive design methodology"), ("E", "Inclusive design also considers situational and temporary limitations, such as a broken arm or bright sunlight glare"), ("F", "Inclusive design replaces the need for WCAG compliance entirely")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [2, 3, 4], explanation: "Inclusive design is a broader design methodology that considers the full range of human diversity and situational or temporary context, while accessibility compliance (like WCAG) is a specific technical standard that inclusive design encompasses but extends beyond. A is incorrect because Treating the terms as identical erases the meaningful distinction in scope between a methodology and a compliance standard. B is incorrect because Both concepts apply across platforms; this artificial split misrepresents the terms. * F: Inclusive design doesn't eliminate the need for WCAG compliance; it works alongside and beyond it."),
        Question(id: "46", question: "A field technician has a temporary broken arm and can only use one hand to interact with a Salesforce mobile app for two weeks. Which concept describes this technician's situation?", options: [("A", "Permanent disability"), ("B", "Information architecture failure"), ("C", "Cognitive overload"), ("D", "Situational disability")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Situational disability refers to a temporary or environmental impairment, such as a temporarily broken arm, that limits ability the same way a permanent disability might, but only for a limited duration or context. A is incorrect because A permanent disability is a lasting condition, whereas this technician's limitation is temporary and will resolve. B is incorrect because Information architecture failure concerns navigation and labeling problems, unrelated to a temporary physical impairment. C is incorrect because Cognitive overload concerns mental processing burden, not a physical, situational limitation."),
        Question(id: "47", question: "Applying the Microsoft Inclusive Design principle \"Solve for One, Extend to Many\" to a Salesforce case console redesign, what should the designer do?", options: [("A", "Design a solution for a specific excluded user (e.g., a one-handed technician) that ends up benefiting the broader user base as well"), ("B", "Design only for the average user and ignore edge cases entirely"), ("C", "Survey the entire user base before making any single design decision"), ("D", "Avoid designing for any specific persona to remain neutral")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "This principle holds that designing specifically for someone facing exclusion (like the one-handed technician) often produces solutions — such as larger touch targets or voice input — that improve usability broadly for everyone else too. B is incorrect because Designing only for the average user is the exclusionary approach this principle explicitly pushes back against. C is incorrect because Surveying the entire user base first isn't the mechanism of this principle, which is about starting from a specific excluded case. D is incorrect because Avoiding any specific persona removes the very starting point (solve for one) that the principle relies on."),
        Question(id: "48", question: "During persona creation, a design team only interviewed headquarters-based sales reps despite the tool being used globally by reps with vastly different market conditions. What risk does this introduce?", options: [("A", "No risk, since headquarters reps represent the company's official brand voice"), ("B", "Bias in the persona set that could lead to a design excluding the needs of non-headquarters users"), ("C", "The personas will automatically self-correct once the tool launches"), ("D", "Persona creation is unaffected by which users are interviewed")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Sourcing persona research from a narrow, unrepresentative group introduces sampling bias that risks producing personas — and downstream design decisions — that don't reflect or serve the needs of the broader, more diverse actual user base. A is incorrect because Brand voice has nothing to do with whether the sampled group represents actual global user needs. C is incorrect because Personas don't self-correct after launch; biased research produces a lasting blind spot unless deliberately revisited. D is incorrect because Persona creation is directly and significantly shaped by who is interviewed, contradicting this claim."),
        Question(id: "49", question: "Which two activities best exemplify applying human-centered design principles when a team has limited access to real end users for a new manager-approval workflow? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "Using a knowledgeable proxy (e.g., a team lead) for contextual walkthroughs"), ("B", "Skipping empathy-building activities entirely to save time"), ("C", "Applying jobs-to-be-done framing to reframe stakeholder feature requests into user goals"), ("D", "Building the highest-fidelity prototype possible before any research"), ("E", "Assuming the design team's own preferences represent user needs")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 2], explanation: "Using a knowledgeable proxy substitutes for direct user access while still grounding decisions in real workflow behavior, and jobs-to-be-done framing ensures feature requests are translated into actual user goals — both are core human-centered techniques usable under access constraints. B is incorrect because Skipping empathy-building entirely abandons the human-centered approach rather than adapting it to constraints. D is incorrect because Building high-fidelity work before any research risks investing heavily in an unvalidated direction. E is incorrect because Substituting the design team's own preferences for user needs is the definition of a non-human-centered approach."),
        Question(id: "50", question: "A service team needs to track equipment repairs where each repair is tied to exactly one customer asset, and the repair record should be deleted automatically if the parent asset is deleted. Which relationship type should the designer recommend?", options: [("A", "Lookup relationship"), ("B", "Many-to-many junction object"), ("C", "Master-detail relationship"), ("D", "Hierarchical custom field")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Master-detail relationships enforce cascading deletes and tie the child record's security to the parent, matching the requirement that the repair record be removed when its parent asset is deleted. A is incorrect because A lookup relationship does not cascade deletes by default, so the repair record would remain an orphan. B is incorrect because A junction object models many-to-many relationships, not the strict one-to-one dependency described here. D is incorrect because A hierarchical field type applies to self-referencing lookups on the same object, not cross-object cascading delete behavior."),
        Question(id: "51", question: "A designer is choosing between a lookup and a master-detail relationship for linking Cases to a custom Warranty Claim object. Stakeholders want Warranty Claims to be able to exist even temporarily without a related Case. Which relationship should be used?", options: [("A", "Master-detail, since it always enforces a required parent"), ("B", "Roll-up summary field, since totals need to be calculated"), ("C", "Junction object, since two objects are involved"), ("D", "Lookup, since it allows the child record's relationship field to be optional")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "A lookup relationship allows the relationship field to be optional or blank, letting a Warranty Claim exist without a Case, unlike master-detail which requires the parent. A is incorrect because Master-detail relationships require a parent record at creation, which conflicts with the stated need for optional linkage. B is incorrect because A roll-up summary field is a calculation feature dependent on master-detail, not a relationship type itself. C is incorrect because A junction object is for many-to-many scenarios, not a simple optional one-to-many link."),
        Question(id: "52", question: "Sales managers want a rolled-up total of all related Opportunity Line Item amounts displayed automatically on the parent Opportunity. Which declarative feature enables this, and what relationship type does it require?", options: [("A", "A roll-up summary field; requires a master-detail relationship"), ("B", "A formula field; requires only a lookup relationship"), ("C", "A validation rule; requires no relationship at all"), ("D", "A compact layout; requires a hierarchical field")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Roll-up summary fields aggregate child record values onto the parent automatically, but this capability is only available across master-detail relationships, not lookups. B is incorrect because A formula field on the parent cannot natively aggregate child records across a lookup relationship without more complex workarounds. C is incorrect because A validation rule enforces data entry conditions; it does not calculate aggregated totals. D is incorrect because A compact layout surfaces key fields in a condensed view; it has no aggregation capability and no dependency on hierarchical fields."),
        Question(id: "53", question: "A nonprofit wants volunteers to be associated with multiple events, and events to have multiple volunteers, while also tracking hours worked per volunteer per event. Which data modeling approach fits best?", options: [("A", "A single lookup field on the Volunteer object pointing to Event"), ("B", "A junction object between Volunteer and Event with a custom Hours field"), ("C", "A roll-up summary field on the Volunteer object"), ("D", "A hierarchical relationship on the Event object")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "A junction object models the many-to-many relationship between Volunteers and Events while also providing a natural place to store per-pairing data like hours worked. A is incorrect because A single lookup field only supports one Volunteer-to-Event pairing per record, not a true many-to-many relationship. C is incorrect because A roll-up summary field aggregates values; it doesn't model the relationship structure needed here. D is incorrect because A hierarchical relationship is for self-referencing records on the same object, not cross-object many-to-many modeling."),
        Question(id: "54", question: "Sales reps want to see both calls logged and meetings scheduled with a customer in one chronological view on the Contact record. Which Salesforce feature natively supports this UX need?", options: [("A", "A validation rule enforcing entry order"), ("B", "A custom object requiring manual chronological sorting"), ("C", "The Activity Timeline showing Tasks and Events together"), ("D", "A dependent picklist showing activity types")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "The Activity Timeline is purpose-built to show Tasks (calls, logged activities) and Events (meetings) together in chronological order directly on the record, satisfying this need out of the box. A is incorrect because A validation rule enforces data entry rules; it doesn't create a chronological display. B is incorrect because A custom object would require rebuilding functionality Salesforce already provides natively through Activities. D is incorrect because A dependent picklist controls field value options, unrelated to timeline display of activities."),
        Question(id: "55", question: "A business wants to rename the \"Opportunity\" object to \"Deal\" throughout the UI to match internal terminology. Which three UX risks should the designer flag before proceeding? (Choose 3)", options: [("A", "Renaming objects is technically impossible in Salesforce"), ("B", "Object renaming requires a developer sandbox and cannot be done declaratively"), ("C", "Renaming an object automatically deletes all existing records"), ("D", "Renamed labels may create confusion when users reference Salesforce Help documentation, which still uses \"Opportunity\""), ("E", "Existing user training materials and internal documentation referencing \"Opportunity\" will become inconsistent"), ("F", "Report and dashboard titles built before the rename may still reference the old label, creating mismatched terminology for users")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [3, 4, 5], explanation: "Since Salesforce Help, Trailhead, training materials, and previously built reports and dashboards all reference the object by its prior name, renaming to internal terminology can create mismatches that confuse users across multiple touchpoints. A is incorrect because Renaming standard object labels is a supported declarative capability, not impossible. B is incorrect because Renaming labels is a declarative Setup change and does not require a sandbox or development work. C is incorrect because Renaming a label has no effect on the underlying data or existing records."),
        Question(id: "56", question: "A designer must decide where to configure the fields and components shown when a user opens a specific Account record versus the fields shown on the Sales app's landing screen. Which two page types are relevant to this distinction? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "Record Home page for the Account detail view"), ("B", "App Home page for the Sales app's landing screen"), ("C", "Login page for authentication"), ("D", "Setup page for object configuration"), ("E", "Report subscription page")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "A Record Home page governs what displays when viewing a specific record like an Account, while an App Home page governs the landing dashboard-style view when a user opens an app, matching the two distinct configuration needs described. C is incorrect because The Login page controls authentication branding, unrelated to record or app landing content. D is incorrect because The Setup page is an administrative configuration area, not a user-facing page type built in Lightning App Builder. E is incorrect because A report subscription page manages scheduled report emails, unrelated to record or app home layout."),
        Question(id: "57", question: "A service agent's record page has grown to over 40 visible fields, most of which are irrelevant to their daily triage task. Which declarative feature best reduces clutter by showing only contextually relevant fields?", options: [("A", "Dynamic Forms with field-level visibility rules"), ("B", "A single long page layout with all fields visible"), ("C", "A validation rule blocking irrelevant fields"), ("D", "Increasing the compact layout's field count")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Dynamic Forms let individual fields be shown or hidden based on record context or user criteria, directly reducing clutter by surfacing only fields relevant to the current triage scenario. B is incorrect because A single long layout with all fields visible is the current problem, not a solution to it. C is incorrect because A validation rule enforces data entry conditions; it cannot hide fields from view. D is incorrect because Increasing compact layout field count adds more visible fields, worsening rather than reducing clutter."),
        Question(id: "58", question: "Which declarative feature should be used to surface the three most critical fields of a custom object in the record highlights panel and in Kanban view cards?", options: [("A", "Dynamic Actions"), ("B", "Compact layout"), ("C", "Path"), ("D", "Validation rule")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Compact layouts control which fields display in condensed views such as the highlights panel, Kanban cards, and mobile, making them the correct tool for surfacing the most critical fields in those contexts. A is incorrect because Dynamic Actions control which buttons appear based on criteria, not which fields display in condensed views. C is incorrect because Path provides stage-based guidance and key field highlighting per stage, but doesn't configure Kanban card or highlights panel fields. D is incorrect because A validation rule enforces data quality rules; it has no bearing on which fields are visually surfaced."),
        Question(id: "59", question: "A sales team manages hundreds of open opportunities and needs to quickly filter by stage, owner, and close date while scanning many records at once. Which declarative feature best supports this?", options: [("A", "A single record's related list"), ("B", "A Path component on the record page"), ("C", "A List View with relevant filters and selected columns"), ("D", "A Quick Action for logging a call")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "List Views with filters and chosen columns are designed exactly for scanning and filtering large volumes of records efficiently, matching this high-volume triage need. A is incorrect because A related list shows child records tied to one parent record, not a filterable view across many independent records. B is incorrect because Path operates on a single record at a time and doesn't support scanning across many records. D is incorrect because A Quick Action performs a single-record task; it isn't a browsing or filtering tool."),
        Question(id: "60", question: "A support team wants to visually manage cases as they move through triage, in-progress, and resolved stages, dragging cards between columns. Which three statements about the best-fit view type are accurate? (Choose 3)", options: [("A", "A standard table list view"), ("B", "A related list"), ("C", "A compact layout"), ("D", "A Kanban view visually represents process stages as columns"), ("E", "Kanban cards can be dragged between columns to update a status or stage field"), ("F", "Kanban views are configured on list views for objects with a status-like field")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [3, 4, 5], explanation: "Kanban views visually represent process stages as columns with draggable cards that update an underlying status field when moved, and are configured on list views for objects that have a suitable status-like field — directly matching the desired drag-and-drop stage management workflow. A is incorrect because A standard table list view displays rows of data but doesn't support visual stage-based drag interactions. B is incorrect because A related list shows child records under a parent, not a stage-based process view. C is incorrect because A compact layout only controls which fields display in a condensed view; it isn't a workflow visualization tool."),
        Question(id: "61", question: "A record page has dense information that must remain accessible but shouldn't all be visible at once, since most users only need one section at a time. Comparing tabs and accordion sections, which should the designer choose if users typically need to reference multiple sections side by side while working?", options: [("A", "Accordion sections, since only one section expands at a time and matches sequential reference needs"), ("B", "Tabs, but only if users need to compare multiple sections simultaneously without collapsing others"), ("C", "Neither; all information should always be shown flat with no grouping"), ("D", "A validation rule to hide unnecessary sections permanently")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Since accordions typically reveal one section at a time while collapsing others, they suit sequential single-section reference; the scenario describes exactly that use case, matching option A's stated behavior. B is incorrect because Tabs also generally show one panel at a time, and the scenario explicitly does not require true multi-section simultaneous comparison, so tabs are not the better fit described here. C is incorrect because A completely flat layout undermines the entire purpose of grouping dense information for manageability. D is incorrect because A validation rule cannot control section visibility; it governs data entry conditions only."),
        Question(id: "62", question: "Users frequently need to view child Contact records from an Account but rarely need to edit them inline. Which declarative approach minimizes navigation while keeping the page uncluttered?", options: [("A", "Embedding every child Contact's full detail page inline"), ("B", "A related list quick link providing a lightweight preview and link to the full related list"), ("C", "A validation rule restricting Contact creation"), ("D", "A Path component tracking Contact status")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "A related list quick link offers a lightweight way to preview and navigate to child records without the overhead of embedding full inline editing capability the users don't need. A is incorrect because Embedding every child Contact's full detail page inline would create excessive clutter for a rarely-edited relationship. C is incorrect because A validation rule affects data entry rules, not navigation or display of related records. D is incorrect because A Path component tracks stage-based progress; it isn't designed for browsing related child records."),
        Question(id: "63", question: "A sales manager wants reps to see which fields matter most at each stage of the opportunity process and receive lightweight coaching guidance as they progress. Which feature should the designer configure?", options: [("A", "A related list"), ("B", "A Kanban view"), ("C", "Sales Path"), ("D", "A compact layout")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Sales Path is specifically designed to highlight key fields and provide stage-based guidance content as a record moves through a process, directly matching this coaching need. A is incorrect because A related list displays child records; it has no stage-based guidance capability. B is incorrect because A Kanban view visualizes records across stages but doesn't provide field highlighting or coaching content per stage. D is incorrect because A compact layout only controls condensed field display; it doesn't provide coaching content."),
        Question(id: "64", question: "A record page shows a \"Close Case\" button even when the case status is already \"Closed,\" confusing agents. Which declarative feature should the designer use to show that button only when relevant?", options: [("A", "A master-detail relationship"), ("B", "A permanent global action available on every page"), ("C", "A roll-up summary field"), ("D", "Dynamic Actions with visibility filters based on the Status field")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Dynamic Actions allow buttons to be shown or hidden based on field-value criteria, so the Close Case button can be configured to only appear when Status is not already Closed. A is incorrect because A master-detail relationship defines a data relationship structure, unrelated to conditional button visibility. B is incorrect because A permanent global action would always display the button regardless of context, which is the current problem. C is incorrect because A roll-up summary field aggregates data; it has no control over action button visibility."),
        Question(id: "65", question: "New users often don't understand why a specific field on the Opportunity form is required or what format it expects. Which three characteristics make field-level help text a good lightweight solution here? (Choose 3)", options: [("A", "It provides just-in-time, contextual guidance exactly where the confusion occurs"), ("B", "A completely separate help website linked from the app menu"), ("C", "A validation rule with no error message"), ("D", "It requires no custom development or Flow to configure"), ("E", "It keeps users on the page instead of navigating away to find an explanation"), ("F", "Removing the field entirely")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 3, 4], explanation: "Field-level help text provides just-in-time, contextual guidance exactly where the confusion occurs, requires no custom development to configure, and keeps users on the page — without requiring users to leave the page or consult external documentation. B is incorrect because A separate help website adds navigation friction and removes the guidance from the point of need. C is incorrect because A validation rule with no error message would block submission without explaining anything, worsening confusion. * F: Removing the field eliminates needed data collection rather than solving the comprehension problem."),
        Question(id: "66", question: "Executives want to see pipeline trends directly while reviewing an Opportunity record, without navigating to a separate analytics tab. Which declarative feature satisfies this?", options: [("A", "A related list showing only open activities"), ("B", "A Report or Dashboard component embedded on the Lightning record page"), ("C", "A Path component"), ("D", "A Quick Action for logging a call")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Embedding a Report or Dashboard component directly on the Lightning page surfaces relevant analytics in context, letting executives see pipeline trends without leaving the record view. A is incorrect because A related list of open activities shows task-level data, not aggregated pipeline trend analytics. C is incorrect because Path shows stage-based guidance, not broader pipeline trend visualizations. D is incorrect because A Quick Action performs a task; it does not display analytics content."),
        Question(id: "67", question: "A global \"New Case\" button needs to be available from any page in the org, regardless of which record the user is currently viewing. Which action type fits this requirement?", options: [("A", "A related list button"), ("B", "An object-specific action tied only to the Case page layout"), ("C", "A global action"), ("D", "A roll-up summary field")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Global actions are available from anywhere in the org, including the global search bar and utility bar, independent of which record or object a user is currently viewing, matching this cross-context requirement. A is incorrect because A related list button appears only within a specific record's related list, not globally. B is incorrect because An object-specific action is scoped to a particular object's context and page layout, not available universally. D is incorrect because A roll-up summary field aggregates data and has no relation to action availability."),
        Question(id: "68", question: "A sales rep must fill out the same five fields every time they log a new lead from a specific campaign. Which declarative feature reduces this repetitive manual entry?", options: [("A", "A master-detail relationship"), ("B", "A validation rule blocking submission until fields are filled"), ("C", "A roll-up summary field"), ("D", "Default field values pre-populated based on record context")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Default field values can pre-populate fields automatically based on context (such as the originating campaign), eliminating repetitive manual entry for predictable values. A is incorrect because A master-detail relationship defines a data structure, not a field pre-population mechanism. B is incorrect because A validation rule only blocks incomplete submissions; it doesn't reduce the manual entry burden itself. C is incorrect because A roll-up summary field aggregates child data; it has no role in pre-populating lead fields."),
        Question(id: "69", question: "A custom object needs a field that automatically calculates the number of days between a Case's Created Date and today, without any user input. Which feature should be used?", options: [("A", "A formula field"), ("B", "A dependent picklist"), ("C", "A validation rule"), ("D", "A Quick Action")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "A formula field automatically calculates and displays a derived value based on other fields, such as computing elapsed days from Created Date, with zero manual input required. B is incorrect because A dependent picklist controls value choices based on another picklist, unrelated to date calculations. C is incorrect because A validation rule enforces conditions on save; it does not calculate or display a running value. D is incorrect because A Quick Action performs a task via a button; it doesn't perform ongoing automatic calculation."),
        Question(id: "70", question: "Users repeatedly select invalid combinations of Country and State on a form, creating bad data. Which declarative feature should the designer configure to prevent invalid combinations while guiding correct selection?", options: [("A", "A formula field calculating the State automatically"), ("B", "A dependent picklist restricting State options based on selected Country"), ("C", "A roll-up summary field"), ("D", "A related list")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Dependent picklists constrain the available options in one field based on the value selected in a controlling field, directly preventing invalid Country/State combinations while guiding the user to valid choices. A is incorrect because A formula field can't reliably reverse-derive a specific State from a Country without additional logic Salesforce doesn't natively support this way. C is incorrect because A roll-up summary field aggregates child record data, unrelated to picklist value control. D is incorrect because A related list displays child records; it has no bearing on picklist dependency logic."),
        Question(id: "71", question: "A designer wants to require that a Discount field is under 20% unless a manager override checkbox is checked, and show a clear error otherwise. Which feature achieves this, and what's the key UX trade-off to weigh?", options: [("A", "A compact layout; trade-off is reduced field visibility only"), ("B", "A formula field; trade-off is slower page load only"), ("C", "A validation rule; trade-off is added friction at save time versus improved data integrity"), ("D", "A Quick Action; trade-off is limited to global context only")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "A validation rule can enforce this conditional business logic at save time, but designers must weigh the friction it introduces against the data integrity benefit it provides. A is incorrect because A compact layout only affects which fields are shown in condensed views, not save-time enforcement. B is incorrect because A formula field calculates and displays values; it cannot block record saves or enforce conditional business rules. D is incorrect because A Quick Action launches a task flow; it isn't a mechanism for conditional field validation."),
        Question(id: "72", question: "New hires must complete a strict, ordered five-screen data collection process for benefits enrollment, and skipping steps is not allowed. Which tool best enforces this rigid sequence?", options: [("A", "A related list"), ("B", "A Guided Action List allowing tasks in any order"), ("C", "A standard page layout with all fields on one page"), ("D", "A Screen Flow with sequential screens")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "A Screen Flow presents a defined, ordered sequence of screens and can enforce that each step is completed before advancing, matching the strict, ordered enrollment process requirement. A is incorrect because A related list displays child records; it has no capability to enforce a data collection sequence. B is incorrect because A Guided Action List is designed for non-linear task completion, which conflicts with the strict ordering requirement here. C is incorrect because A single-page layout with all fields presents everything at once with no enforced sequence or step gating."),
        Question(id: "73", question: "A screen in a Flow should show a discount approval field only if the entered discount exceeds 15%, otherwise it should stay hidden to reduce clutter. Which Flow capability should be used instead of building two separate screens?", options: [("A", "Conditional visibility on the Screen component"), ("B", "A separate Flow entirely for high discounts"), ("C", "A roll-up summary field"), ("D", "A dependent picklist")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Conditional visibility lets a field or component appear or hide within the same screen based on a condition, avoiding the need to branch into duplicate screens for a single differing field. B is incorrect because Building an entirely separate Flow duplicates maintenance effort for what is a single conditional field difference. C is incorrect because A roll-up summary field aggregates child records; it has no role in screen-level conditional display. D is incorrect because A dependent picklist controls picklist option availability, not general field visibility based on a numeric threshold."),
        Question(id: "74", question: "Field technicians need to complete a checklist of tasks across multiple site visits over several days, in any order, checking items off as they go. Which tool fits better than a strict sequential Screen Flow?", options: [("A", "A single-screen Flow with no save capability"), ("B", "A Guided Action List"), ("C", "A validation rule"), ("D", "A roll-up summary field")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "A Guided Action List supports completing tasks non-linearly and across multiple sessions, which matches technicians checking off items over several days in any order, unlike a rigid sequential Screen Flow. A is incorrect because A single-screen Flow without save capability can't support work spanning multiple days or sessions. C is incorrect because A validation rule only enforces save-time conditions; it has no checklist or task-tracking capability. D is incorrect because A roll-up summary field aggregates numeric data; it isn't a task-tracking tool."),
        Question(id: "75", question: "A manager wants a Slack-style reminder Flow that automatically populates the Next Steps field with a follow-up date whenever an Opportunity stage changes to \"Negotiation,\" without the rep doing anything. Which automation approach fits?", options: [("A", "A compact layout"), ("B", "A Quick Action requiring the rep to click a button"), ("C", "A record-triggered Flow that runs automatically on the stage change"), ("D", "A dependent picklist")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "A record-triggered Flow runs automatically in the background when the specified condition (stage change to Negotiation) occurs, requiring no manual action from the rep, matching this hands-off requirement. A is incorrect because A compact layout only affects field display in condensed views; it has no automation capability. B is incorrect because A Quick Action requires explicit rep interaction, contradicting the \"without the rep doing anything\" requirement. D is incorrect because A dependent picklist governs option selection, not automated field population on a triggered event."),
        Question(id: "76", question: "Service agents handling a common complaint type must currently update five fields and send a templated email manually every time. Which Service Cloud feature reduces this to a single click?", options: [("A", "A master-detail relationship"), ("B", "A dependent picklist"), ("C", "A roll-up summary field"), ("D", "A Macro executing the multi-step sequence automatically")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "A Macro can bundle multiple repetitive steps, such as field updates and sending a templated email, into a single agent-triggered action, directly reducing the manual repetitive burden described. A is incorrect because A master-detail relationship defines record hierarchy, not an action automation mechanism. B is incorrect because A dependent picklist only constrains field value options; it cannot execute a multi-step action sequence. C is incorrect because A roll-up summary field aggregates numeric totals, unrelated to executing a task sequence."),
        Question(id: "77", question: "A regional VP needs a Lightning App configured so that their home screen surfaces pipeline dashboards and their top navigation items match executive priorities, distinct from a rep's daily console. Which two configuration elements should the designer set for this app? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "Custom navigation items reflecting executive priorities"), ("B", "A branded App Home page with pipeline dashboard components"), ("C", "A dependent picklist for Opportunity Stage"), ("D", "A validation rule blocking Stage changes"), ("E", "A roll-up summary field on Contact")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Configuring custom navigation items and a branded App Home page with dashboard components directly aligns the app's structure and landing content with executive priorities, distinct from a rep-focused console. C is incorrect because A dependent picklist governs picklist value logic, unrelated to app navigation or home page configuration. D is incorrect because A validation rule enforces data entry conditions, not app-level navigation or dashboard presentation. E is incorrect because A roll-up summary field aggregates Contact-related data; it has no bearing on app configuration for an executive persona."),
        Question(id: "78", question: "A service agent needs to work on multiple cases simultaneously, referencing a knowledge article in a side panel while a case detail remains pinned. Which Lightning navigation model fits best?", options: [("A", "The Salesforce Console app with split view and pinned regions"), ("B", "Standard single-tab navigation"), ("C", "A single record's compact layout"), ("D", "A global action")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "The Console app navigation model supports multiple open tabs, split views, and pinned regions, enabling an agent to reference a knowledge article alongside a pinned case detail simultaneously. B is incorrect because Standard single-tab navigation only shows one primary view at a time, without the split-view or pinning capability needed here. C is incorrect because A compact layout only governs condensed field display; it isn't a navigation model. D is incorrect because A global action performs a task; it doesn't provide a multi-pane working environment."),
        Question(id: "79", question: "Reps frequently need quick access to their notes-taking tool and a softphone dialer without leaving their current record. Which feature should the designer configure?", options: [("A", "A related list on every object"), ("B", "The utility bar with pinned Notes and Open CTI components"), ("C", "A validation rule"), ("D", "A roll-up summary field")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "The utility bar persists across the console and can house frequently used tools like Notes and Open CTI, giving reps quick access without navigating away from their current record. A is incorrect because A related list is scoped to a specific record's child data; it can't provide a persistent cross-page utility tool. C is incorrect because A validation rule enforces data entry conditions; it has no bearing on persistent tool access. D is incorrect because A roll-up summary field aggregates data; it isn't a UI access mechanism."),
        Question(id: "80", question: "Users report that global search often returns irrelevant object types cluttering their results when they search for a customer name. Which configuration should the designer review to improve this?", options: [("A", "A roll-up summary field"), ("B", "A dependent picklist configuration"), ("C", "Search Layouts and object search settings governing which objects and fields are searched"), ("D", "A master-detail relationship")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Search Layouts and the org's search settings determine which objects and fields participate in global search results, so tuning them directly addresses irrelevant result clutter. A is incorrect because A roll-up summary field aggregates numeric totals; it has no role in search behavior. B is incorrect because A dependent picklist governs field value dependencies, unrelated to search scope configuration. D is incorrect because A master-detail relationship defines a data hierarchy, not search configuration."),
        Question(id: "81", question: "New employees consistently miss a required annual compliance training announcement inside a specific Lightning page. Which In-App Guidance mechanism should the designer use to ensure it's seen without being dismissible after one glance?", options: [("A", "A validation rule"), ("B", "A targeted prompt appearing only on hover"), ("C", "A Trailhead module unrelated to the page"), ("D", "A docked prompt that remains visible on the page rather than a single floating tooltip")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "A docked prompt stays persistently visible within the page layout rather than disappearing after a quick glance, making it a better fit for ensuring a mandatory message is actually seen. A is incorrect because A validation rule enforces data entry rules; it has no messaging or guidance capability. B is incorrect because A targeted prompt on hover is easily missed if the user never hovers over the specific element, unlike a persistently docked prompt. C is incorrect because A Trailhead module lives outside the page and won't intercept in-context attention the way in-app guidance does."),
        Question(id: "82", question: "A major new feature is rolling out that changes how users complete a multi-step approval process. Which In-App Guidance format best introduces this changed multi-step process?", options: [("A", "A multi-step Walkthrough guiding users through the new process"), ("B", "A single floating prompt shown once"), ("C", "A field-level help text tooltip"), ("D", "A generic help menu link")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "A multi-step Walkthrough can guide users sequentially through each part of a changed multi-step process, which is more effective for substantial process changes than a single static prompt. B is incorrect because A single floating prompt shown once cannot adequately explain a multi-step process change. C is incorrect because Field-level help text addresses one field at a time, not an end-to-end process change. D is incorrect because A generic help menu link requires users to seek it out actively rather than guiding them proactively through the change."),
        Question(id: "83", question: "Employees ask where to find role-specific onboarding material without cluttering the main interface with permanent banners. Which configuration should the designer use?", options: [("A", "A permanent banner pinned to every page"), ("B", "Custom help menu items linking to role-specific Salesforce Help and Training resources"), ("C", "A validation rule"), ("D", "A roll-up summary field")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Custom help menu items let the designer surface role-specific resources within the existing Help menu, giving users a discoverable path to onboarding material without adding permanent visual clutter. A is incorrect because A permanent banner adds ongoing visual clutter, which the requirement explicitly wants to avoid. C is incorrect because A validation rule enforces save-time conditions; it has no role in surfacing help resources. D is incorrect because A roll-up summary field aggregates data; it is unrelated to onboarding resource delivery."),
        Question(id: "84", question: "A company wants to deliver in-depth, self-paced skill-building content branded with their own logo for ongoing Salesforce feature adoption, beyond simple in-app tips. Which tool is most appropriate?", options: [("A", "A validation rule"), ("B", "A single targeted prompt"), ("C", "myTrailhead"), ("D", "A compact layout")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "myTrailhead enables organizations to deliver branded, in-depth, self-paced learning content for ongoing skill-building, which goes beyond the scope of lightweight in-app prompts. A is incorrect because A validation rule enforces data entry rules; it has no learning delivery capability. B is incorrect because A single targeted prompt offers only brief, contextual guidance, not in-depth self-paced learning content. D is incorrect because A compact layout controls condensed field display; it is unrelated to learning content delivery."),
        Question(id: "85", question: "A company's brand guidelines specify a primary color, logo, and typography that must appear consistently across the internal Salesforce org. Which declarative feature applies this?", options: [("A", "A master-detail relationship"), ("B", "A validation rule"), ("C", "A roll-up summary field"), ("D", "A custom Lightning Experience theme with brand color, logo, and default branding image")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Custom Lightning Experience themes let admins declaratively configure brand colors, logos, and default images to align the org's look with corporate brand guidelines. A is incorrect because A master-detail relationship defines a data structure, not a branding mechanism. B is incorrect because A validation rule enforces data entry conditions, unrelated to visual branding. C is incorrect because A roll-up summary field aggregates numeric data; it has no visual branding capability."),
        Question(id: "86", question: "A company's external partner portal must reflect the same brand colors and fonts as the internal org, configured entirely without code. Which declarative tool should the designer use?", options: [("A", "Experience Builder's branding panel for header, footer, colors, and fonts"), ("B", "A Screen Flow"), ("C", "A validation rule"), ("D", "A roll-up summary field")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Experience Builder's branding panel provides a no-code interface for configuring the external site's header, footer, color palette, and fonts to align with brand standards. B is incorrect because A Screen Flow builds guided data-entry processes; it isn't a branding configuration tool. C is incorrect because A validation rule enforces data entry conditions, unrelated to site branding. D is incorrect because A roll-up summary field aggregates data, unrelated to site appearance."),
        Question(id: "87", question: "A designer wants to apply a highly specific brand gradient that Experience Builder's declarative branding panel cannot produce. What should the designer do?", options: [("A", "Abandon the gradient requirement entirely with no further discussion"), ("B", "Escalate to a developer to implement custom CSS or SLDS token overrides"), ("C", "Use a validation rule to enforce the gradient"), ("D", "Use a roll-up summary field to calculate the gradient colors")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "When declarative branding tools can't achieve a specific visual requirement, escalating to a developer for custom CSS or SLDS token overrides is the appropriate next step within platform constraints. A is incorrect because Abandoning the requirement skips a viable escalation path that could still meet the need. C is incorrect because A validation rule enforces data conditions; it has no visual styling capability whatsoever. D is incorrect because A roll-up summary field aggregates numeric values; it cannot produce visual gradients."),
        Question(id: "88", question: "Field agents use a Salesforce console app in bright outdoor daylight for long shifts, and report eye strain from the default light theme. What UX consideration should the designer raise about theme choice?", options: [("A", "Dark themes are banned by Salesforce platform policy"), ("B", "Theme choice has no measurable impact on any user's experience"), ("C", "A dark theme could reduce glare and eye strain in certain lighting conditions, but tradeoffs with outdoor readability should be tested with users"), ("D", "Light themes are always superior regardless of environment")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Different lighting environments affect how comfortable a given theme is to use, so testing a dark theme option against outdoor readability needs is a reasonable, evidence-based response rather than assuming one theme is universally best. A is incorrect because There is no such platform-wide ban on dark themes; this is a fabricated constraint. B is incorrect because Theme choice does affect visual comfort and legibility, especially in high-glare outdoor settings, contradicting this claim. D is incorrect because Asserting light themes are always superior ignores the specific outdoor glare problem described and needs validation, not assumption."),
        Question(id: "89", question: "A researcher is designing usability test tasks for a new expense report submission flow. Which task-writing practice best avoids biasing participant behavior?", options: [("A", "\"Please click the blue Submit button to finish\""), ("B", "\"Rate this flow from 1 to 10\""), ("C", "\"Do you think this flow is easy to use?\""), ("D", "\"You just finished a business trip and need to submit your expenses for reimbursement — please do that now\"")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "A realistic, goal-based scenario without naming specific UI elements lets the participant discover the path naturally, revealing authentic usability issues rather than confirming a predetermined path. A is incorrect because Naming the exact button to click removes any discovery element and can't reveal whether users could find it themselves. B is incorrect because A numeric rating captures perception after the fact, not task performance during the session. C is incorrect because This is a subjective opinion question, not a task, and doesn't observe actual behavior."),
        Question(id: "90", question: "A team is recruiting participants for a usability test of an internal HR self-service tool. Which screening criterion best avoids bias from familiarity with the product?", options: [("A", "Recruit employees who have never used the current HR tool but match the target persona's role and tech comfort level"), ("B", "Recruit only employees who helped design the tool"), ("C", "Recruit only the IT help desk staff"), ("D", "Recruit whoever is available in the office that day regardless of role")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Matching the target persona's role and comfort level while excluding people already familiar with the product avoids both irrelevant feedback and bias from insider familiarity. B is incorrect because People who helped design the tool already have insider knowledge that would bias their navigation and feedback. C is incorrect because IT help desk staff are likely to have unusually high technical familiarity, not representative of the broader employee base. D is incorrect because Recruiting whoever is available ignores whether participants actually represent the target user population."),
        Question(id: "91", question: "A team needs to catch major usability issues in a new onboarding flow within one week, with a tight budget. How many participants should they plan to recruit per round?", options: [("A", "200 participants for statistical significance"), ("B", "Roughly five to seven participants, sufficient to surface most major usability issues"), ("C", "One participant, since more would be redundant"), ("D", "Fifty participants to be thorough")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Usability research shows that testing with around five to seven participants typically surfaces the majority of major usability issues, making it an efficient fit for a tight budget and timeline focused on qualitative issue-finding rather than statistical measurement. A is incorrect because 200 participants is suited to quantitative statistical confidence, not quick qualitative issue-finding, and is impractical within a week. C is incorrect because A single participant risks missing issues that only a few different users would encounter. D is incorrect because Fifty participants exceeds what's needed for qualitative issue discovery and wastes time and budget."),
        Question(id: "92", question: "Users are spread across multiple time zones and a facilitator wants to actively probe their reasoning as they navigate a new feature in real time. Which testing format fits best?", options: [("A", "Unmoderated remote testing"), ("B", "In-person moderated testing only"), ("C", "Moderated remote testing"), ("D", "A mailed paper survey")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Moderated remote testing lets the facilitator actively probe reasoning in real time while accommodating participants across different locations and time zones. A is incorrect because Unmoderated testing removes the facilitator's ability to probe reasoning live during the session. B is incorrect because In-person-only testing doesn't scale across geographically distributed time zones without heavy travel cost. D is incorrect because A mailed paper survey collects static responses, not real-time behavioral or verbal reasoning data."),
        Question(id: "93", question: "A designer has a rough paper sketch of a new navigation concept and wants very fast, low-cost feedback on the overall structure before building anything digital. Which method fits best?", options: [("A", "A formal moderated usability test with a signed consent process and video recording"), ("B", "A statistically powered A/B test"), ("C", "A twelve-week diary study"), ("D", "Guerrilla (hallway) testing with a handful of quick, informal participants")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Guerrilla testing provides fast, low-cost, informal feedback appropriate for an early paper sketch, without the overhead of formal recruitment or lengthy setup. A is incorrect because A formal moderated test with full consent and recording processes adds overhead disproportionate to validating a rough early sketch. B is incorrect because A/B testing requires live deployable variants, which don't exist for a paper sketch. C is incorrect because A twelve-week diary study is far too slow and heavyweight for quick early-stage feedback."),
        Question(id: "94", question: "A team wants to validate whether users understand the purpose of a new landing page within the first moments of viewing it, before any interaction. Which testing method fits best?", options: [("A", "A five-second test"), ("B", "A twelve-week diary study"), ("C", "A System Usability Scale survey"), ("D", "A think-aloud protocol during a multi-step task")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "A five-second test shows users a design for a brief moment and asks what they understood, directly measuring immediate comprehension of a landing page's purpose. B is incorrect because A twelve-week diary study measures behavior over time, not immediate first-glance comprehension. C is incorrect because A SUS survey measures perceived usability after interacting with a system, not immediate visual comprehension. D is incorrect because A think-aloud protocol during a multi-step task observes ongoing reasoning, not first-glance impression."),
        Question(id: "95", question: "A team wants to improve a prototype iteratively while it's still being built, catching usability issues before launch. What type of testing is this?", options: [("A", "Summative testing"), ("B", "Formative testing"), ("C", "User Acceptance Testing"), ("D", "A/B testing in production")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Formative testing is conducted during development specifically to catch and fix usability issues iteratively before the design is finalized, matching this scenario exactly. A is incorrect because Summative testing evaluates a completed design against a benchmark, not an in-progress iterative improvement process. C is incorrect because UAT confirms a solution meets business/functional requirements, not usability improvement during design iteration. D is incorrect because A/B testing in production requires a live, finished deployment, not an in-progress prototype."),
        Question(id: "96", question: "A Salesforce implementation partner wants business stakeholders to confirm the new quoting configuration meets documented business requirements before go-live. Which activity is this, and how does it differ from a UX usability test?", options: [("A", "A think-aloud protocol; it captures verbalized reasoning"), ("B", "A five-second test; it measures first impressions"), ("C", "User Acceptance Testing; it validates functional requirements rather than measuring usability or user experience quality"), ("D", "A SUS survey; it produces a numeric usability score")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "UAT confirms that a solution satisfies documented business and functional requirements, which is a different goal from a UX usability test's focus on how easily and effectively users can actually use the design. A is incorrect because A think-aloud protocol captures a participant's real-time reasoning during a task, not a formal requirements confirmation process. B is incorrect because A five-second test measures first-glance comprehension, unrelated to formal business requirement sign-off. D is incorrect because A SUS survey quantifies perceived usability, not formal business requirement validation."),
        Question(id: "97", question: "During a usability session, the facilitator asks the participant to narrate their thoughts continuously as they attempt each step of a new checkout flow. Which technique is this?", options: [("A", "A five-second test"), ("B", "Co-discovery"), ("C", "Retrospective probing"), ("D", "Think-aloud protocol")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Think-aloud protocol asks participants to verbalize their thoughts continuously while performing tasks, capturing real-time reasoning as it happens. A is incorrect because A five-second test measures brief first-impression comprehension, not ongoing narrated reasoning during a task. B is incorrect because Co-discovery involves two participants working together and discussing with each other, not a single participant narrating alone. C is incorrect because Retrospective probing occurs after the task is complete, asking participants to recall their reasoning, not during the task itself."),
        Question(id: "98", question: "Two participants work together on the same task, discussing and debating their reasoning with each other while the facilitator observes. Which usability technique is this?", options: [("A", "Co-discovery"), ("B", "Concurrent probing"), ("C", "A SUS questionnaire"), ("D", "A/B testing")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Co-discovery pairs two participants to work through a task together, surfacing rich discussion and differing perspectives that a single-participant session wouldn't reveal. B is incorrect because Concurrent probing involves the facilitator asking questions during a single participant's task, not two participants discussing between themselves. C is incorrect because A SUS questionnaire is a post-session survey instrument, not a live paired-observation technique. D is incorrect because A/B testing compares two live design variants statistically, unrelated to paired qualitative observation."),
        Question(id: "99", question: "After a usability test session, participants complete a standardized ten-item questionnaire, and the team calculates a score to benchmark perceived usability against previous rounds. Which tool is this, and how should a low score be interpreted?", options: [("A", "A five-second test; a low score means the color palette must change"), ("B", "The System Usability Scale; a low score signals lower perceived usability warranting further investigation into specific pain points"), ("C", "A think-aloud protocol; a low score is not calculable"), ("D", "A journey map; a low score means the map needs redrawing")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "The System Usability Scale is a standardized ten-item questionnaire producing a comparable usability score, and a low score indicates perceived usability issues that merit deeper qualitative investigation rather than being a final verdict alone. A is incorrect because A five-second test measures first-glance comprehension, not a scored questionnaire, and doesn't imply anything about color specifically. C is incorrect because A think-aloud protocol is a qualitative observation technique, not a scored questionnaire at all. D is incorrect because A journey map is a synthesis artifact, not a scored usability instrument."),
        Question(id: "100", question: "A researcher must present usability findings to stakeholders who did not observe any sessions and have limited time. Which reporting approach communicates severity and priority most effectively?", options: [("A", "A raw, unedited transcript of every session with no summary"), ("B", "A single overall pass/fail verdict with no supporting detail"), ("C", "A structured report with severity ratings, recurring patterns, and prioritized recommendations tied to evidence"), ("D", "An informal verbal recap with no written documentation")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Structuring findings by severity, highlighting recurring patterns, and tying prioritized recommendations to evidence gives time-constrained stakeholders a clear, actionable picture without requiring them to review raw session data. A is incorrect because Raw unedited transcripts require significant time investment from stakeholders to extract meaning, defeating the purpose of a report. B is incorrect because A single pass/fail verdict provides no actionable detail about what to fix or why. D is incorrect because An informal verbal recap with no documentation is not persistent or precise enough to guide prioritization decisions later."),
        Question(id: "101", question: "Two usability findings emerge from the same test: one prevents task completion entirely for most participants, and another is a minor visual misalignment noticed by one participant. How should severity be assigned, and what does that imply for launch prioritization?", options: [("A", "Both are equally critical and must be fixed before launch"), ("B", "Severity ratings are not useful for prioritization decisions"), ("C", "The visual misalignment should be rated critical since it was noticed first"), ("D", "The task-blocking issue should be rated critical or serious and fixed before launch; the visual misalignment should be rated cosmetic and can be deferred")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Severity ratings should reflect actual impact — an issue blocking task completion for most participants is critical or serious and warrants pre-launch fixing, while a cosmetic, low-impact issue noticed by only one participant can reasonably be deferred to a later release. A is incorrect because Treating both issues as equally critical ignores the vastly different impact and participant frequency between them. B is incorrect because Severity ratings exist specifically to support prioritization decisions like this one; dismissing their usefulness contradicts their purpose. C is incorrect because Rating the cosmetic issue as critical based on discovery order rather than actual impact misapplies the severity framework."),
        Question(id: "102", question: "A developer building a custom LWC asks why they should bother with SLDS at all if the component already works functionally. What is the core purpose of SLDS the designer should explain?", options: [("A", "SLDS provides a shared visual language and CSS framework ensuring the custom component looks consistent with native Lightning Experience elements"), ("B", "SLDS automatically writes all Apex logic needed for the component"), ("C", "SLDS replaces the need for Lightning Web Components entirely"), ("D", "SLDS is only relevant for Experience Cloud sites, not internal orgs")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "SLDS exists to give custom components the same visual language, spacing, and styling as native Lightning elements, so users experience visual consistency across the platform regardless of who built each piece. B is incorrect because SLDS is a visual/CSS specification, not a logic or business-rule framework; it has no bearing on Apex. C is incorrect because SLDS is a styling system, not a component framework, and doesn't replace LWC or Aura. D is incorrect because SLDS applies across Lightning Experience broadly, not just Experience Cloud."),
        Question(id: "103", question: "A developer assumes SLDS will automatically generate the JavaScript behavior for a custom interactive component. What should the designer clarify about what SLDS does NOT provide?", options: [("A", "SLDS provides full interactive behavior for every component automatically"), ("B", "SLDS is a CSS framework and visual specification; it does not provide JavaScript logic or interactive behavior"), ("C", "SLDS is a replacement for all custom development"), ("D", "SLDS only works with Aura components, not LWC")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "SLDS defines visual styling, tokens, and markup patterns, but developers must still implement the actual interactive JavaScript behavior themselves; SLDS does not generate functional logic. A is incorrect because This contradicts the reality that SLDS is purely visual/CSS, not a behavior-generating framework. C is incorrect because SLDS supplements custom development with consistent styling; it doesn't eliminate the need for development work. D is incorrect because SLDS is intended to work with both LWC and Aura component styling, not exclusively one framework."),
        Question(id: "104", question: "A team is deciding whether their custom LWC needs manual SLDS styling applied. Which statement correctly distinguishes base LWC components from fully custom ones regarding SLDS?", options: [("A", "SLDS styling only applies to Aura components, never LWC"), ("B", "All LWC components, base or custom, automatically receive SLDS styling with no developer effort"), ("C", "Base LWC components (like lightning-button) automatically apply SLDS styling, while fully custom markup requires manually applying SLDS classes"), ("D", "Custom LWC markup never needs any SLDS styling applied")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Salesforce's base Lightning components come pre-styled with SLDS out of the box, but any custom HTML markup a developer writes inside an LWC must have SLDS classes manually applied to match the platform's look. A is incorrect because SLDS styling is relevant to both LWC and Aura components, not exclusive to Aura. B is incorrect because Custom markup within an LWC does not automatically inherit SLDS styling without manual class application. D is incorrect because Custom markup absolutely benefits from and typically requires SLDS styling to match platform consistency."),
        Question(id: "105", question: "A designer wants a custom component's spacing and colors to automatically update if the org's global visual standards change later, without manually rewriting CSS values everywhere. Which SLDS concept enables this?", options: [("A", "A validation rule"), ("B", "SLDS component blueprints"), ("C", "SLDS utility classes only"), ("D", "SLDS design tokens")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Design tokens are named variables representing visual values (like color and spacing) that propagate globally, so updating a token's definition cascades the change everywhere it's referenced without manual rewrites. A is incorrect because A validation rule enforces data entry conditions; it has no relationship to visual styling propagation. B is incorrect because Component blueprints are markup/structure specifications for building specific UI patterns, not a mechanism for propagating global value changes. C is incorrect because Utility classes apply fixed, single-purpose styling and don't inherently propagate centrally-managed value changes the way tokens do."),
        Question(id: "106", question: "A developer needs to quickly add consistent spacing and text alignment to a custom component without writing new CSS rules. Which SLDS resource fits this need?", options: [("A", "SLDS utility classes"), ("B", "SLDS component blueprints"), ("C", "A roll-up summary field"), ("D", "A validation rule")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "SLDS utility classes provide pre-built, single-purpose classes for spacing, alignment, text, and layout that can be applied directly in markup without writing custom CSS. B is incorrect because Component blueprints describe full markup patterns for complex UI elements, not simple, atomic utility styling. C is incorrect because A roll-up summary field aggregates data; it has no relationship to visual styling. D is incorrect because A validation rule enforces save-time conditions, unrelated to CSS styling utilities."),
        Question(id: "107", question: "A developer needs a detailed markup and structural reference for building a custom modal dialog that doesn't have a base LWC equivalent. Which SLDS resource should they consult?", options: [("A", "SLDS design tokens"), ("B", "SLDS component blueprints"), ("C", "A roll-up summary field"), ("D", "A dependent picklist")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Component blueprints provide the exact markup structure and class specifications needed to build a UI pattern like a modal from scratch while matching native Lightning Experience appearance. A is incorrect because Design tokens define individual visual values like colors and spacing, not full structural markup patterns. C is incorrect because A roll-up summary field aggregates data; it has no bearing on component structure. D is incorrect because A dependent picklist controls field value logic, unrelated to component markup specification."),
        Question(id: "108", question: "A designer needs to display tabular data with inline editing capability in a custom Lightning page. Which approach best follows SLDS-consistent, out-of-the-box guidance?", options: [("A", "Use a validation rule to render the table"), ("B", "Build a fully custom HTML table with manually written CSS from scratch"), ("C", "Use the lightning-datatable base component with its inline-edit variant"), ("D", "Use a roll-up summary field to display the data")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "The lightning-datatable base component already includes SLDS styling and supports an inline-editing variant out of the box, avoiding the need to hand-build and manually style a custom table. A is incorrect because A validation rule enforces data entry conditions; it cannot render a data table. B is incorrect because Building a fully custom table from scratch duplicates functionality Salesforce already provides and risks visual inconsistency without careful manual SLDS application. D is incorrect because A roll-up summary field aggregates numeric totals; it has no table rendering capability."),
        Question(id: "109", question: "A custom component needs an icon representing a custom object type, distinct from standard action or utility icons. Which SLDS icon category should be used?", options: [("A", "Doctype icon category"), ("B", "Utility icon category"), ("C", "Action icon category"), ("D", "Custom icon category")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "The custom icon category is specifically designated for representing custom objects and org-specific concepts, distinct from generic utility or action icons. A is incorrect because Doctype icons represent file types, unrelated to representing a custom object. B is incorrect because Utility icons represent generic interface actions and concepts, not custom object identity. C is incorrect because Action icons represent specific user actions (like \"new\" or \"edit\"), not object type identity."),
        Question(id: "110", question: "Which SLDS icon category should a designer use to represent a file attachment's type, such as a PDF or Word document, within a custom component?", options: [("A", "Doctype icon category"), ("B", "Standard icon category"), ("C", "Custom icon category"), ("D", "Action icon category")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "The doctype icon category is specifically designed to visually represent file types like PDFs and Word documents, matching this exact use case. B is incorrect because Standard icons represent standard Salesforce objects (like Account or Contact), not file types. C is incorrect because Custom icons represent custom objects specific to an org, not generic file type indicators. D is incorrect because Action icons represent user-initiated actions, unrelated to file type representation."),
        Question(id: "111", question: "A data table in a custom component needs to let users perform a row-specific action, like \"View Details,\" from a dropdown menu next to each row. Which SLDS data table variant addresses this?", options: [("A", "A read-only data table with no interactive elements"), ("B", "A data table with row actions"), ("C", "An inline-editing data table with no row actions"), ("D", "A data table with only column sorting enabled")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "A data table with row actions provides the dropdown menu structure for per-row actions like \"View Details,\" directly matching this requirement. A is incorrect because A read-only table has no interactive elements at all, which conflicts with the need for a row action dropdown. C is incorrect because An inline-editing table variant focuses on editing cell values, not launching row-specific actions via a dropdown. D is incorrect because A sorting-only table variant addresses column reordering, not row-level action menus."),
        Question(id: "112", question: "A developer is deciding whether to style a custom LWC using SLDS utility classes or write scoped CSS in the component's own stylesheet. When is writing scoped CSS the better choice?", options: [("A", "Never, since SLDS utility classes cover every possible styling need"), ("B", "Always, regardless of whether SLDS utilities could achieve the same result"), ("C", "When the required visual treatment is highly specific and not covered by any existing SLDS utility class or token"), ("D", "Only when the component has no visual requirements at all")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Scoped CSS is appropriate when a specific visual requirement genuinely isn't covered by existing SLDS utilities or tokens; otherwise, reusing SLDS utilities keeps styling consistent and reduces maintenance overhead. * A: SLDS utility classes don't cover every conceivable styling need; highly specific cases can still require custom CSS. * B: Defaulting to custom CSS even when SLDS utilities would suffice increases inconsistency risk and maintenance burden unnecessarily. * D: A component with no visual requirements wouldn't need any styling decision in the first place, making this scenario nonsensical. * B: Defaulting to custom CSS even when SLDS utilities would suffice increases inconsistency risk and maintenance burden unnecessarily. * C: SLDS utility classes don't cover every conceivable styling need; highly specific cases can still require custom CSS. * D: A component with no visual requirements wouldn't need any styling decision in the first place, making this scenario nonsensical."),
        Question(id: "113", question: "A designer wants a custom component's colors to update automatically if the org later adopts a different brand palette, without touching the component's CSS file directly. Which approach achieves this?", options: [("A", "Creating a roll-up summary field to store the color value"), ("B", "Hardcoding hex color values directly into the CSS file"), ("C", "Writing a validation rule referencing the desired colors"), ("D", "Applying SLDS design tokens to override default visual styles, rather than hardcoding color values")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Using SLDS design tokens rather than hardcoded values means future palette changes only require updating the token definitions, automatically cascading to every component referencing them. A is incorrect because A roll-up summary field aggregates numeric data; it cannot store or apply styling tokens. B is incorrect because Hardcoded hex values require manual updates in every file whenever the palette changes, the opposite of the desired outcome. C is incorrect because A validation rule enforces data entry logic; it has no capability to define or apply visual color values."),
        Question(id: "114", question: "No base LWC exists for a highly specific custom progress-tracker widget the design calls for. Which SLDS resource should the developer use as a reference to keep it visually consistent with Lightning Experience?", options: [("A", "SLDS Blueprints as a structural and visual reference specification"), ("B", "A roll-up summary field"), ("C", "A validation rule"), ("D", "A dependent picklist")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "When no base LWC exists for a needed pattern, SLDS Blueprints provide the reference markup and visual specification needed to build a custom implementation that still looks native to Lightning Experience. B is incorrect because A roll-up summary field aggregates data values; it has no bearing on visual component construction. C is incorrect because A validation rule enforces save-time data conditions, unrelated to visual component design. D is incorrect because A dependent picklist governs picklist option logic, unrelated to building a custom visual widget."),
        Question(id: "115", question: "A custom component's expandable section needs to visually indicate whether it is currently expanded or collapsed. Which SLDS mechanism should represent this dynamic state?", options: [("A", "SLDS design tokens"), ("B", "SLDS state classes such as is-open"), ("C", "A validation rule"), ("D", "A roll-up summary field")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "SLDS state classes like is-open and is-active are specifically designed to reflect dynamic UI states such as expanded/collapsed, toggling styling based on the component's current condition. A is incorrect because Design tokens represent static visual values like colors and spacing, not toggled interactive states. C is incorrect because A validation rule enforces save-time data conditions; it has no role in visual state representation. D is incorrect because A roll-up summary field aggregates data; it cannot represent a dynamic UI toggle state."),
        Question(id: "116", question: "A custom form component needs to show a red asterisk next to required fields and an inline error message below any field that fails validation, consistent with native Salesforce forms. Which SLDS resource should guide this?", options: [("A", "A roll-up summary field"), ("B", "SLDS component blueprints for data tables"), ("C", "SLDS validation patterns for required field indicators and inline error messages"), ("D", "A master-detail relationship")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "SLDS validation patterns define the standard visual treatment for required-field indicators and inline error messaging, ensuring a custom form matches the accessible, familiar look of native Salesforce forms. A is incorrect because A roll-up summary field aggregates data; it has no bearing on form validation UI patterns. B is incorrect because Data table blueprints address tabular data presentation, not form-level validation styling. D is incorrect because A master-detail relationship defines data structure, unrelated to form validation styling."),
        Question(id: "117", question: "A custom component takes several seconds to load data from an external system. Which SLDS pattern should be used to prevent users from thinking the interface is broken during that wait?", options: [("A", "A validation rule"), ("B", "SLDS state classes for expanded/collapsed sections"), ("C", "A roll-up summary field"), ("D", "SLDS loading spinner or skeleton screen pattern")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Loading spinners and skeleton screens communicate that the system is actively processing, preventing users from mistaking a slow load for a broken interface. A is incorrect because A validation rule enforces save-time conditions, unrelated to communicating a loading state. B is incorrect because State classes for expand/collapse address a different kind of UI state, not loading/processing feedback. C is incorrect because A roll-up summary field aggregates data values; it has no relevance to loading state communication."),
        Question(id: "118", question: "Which two considerations should guide whether a designer uses a standard SLDS badge/pill component versus building fully custom markup for a status indicator? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "Whether the existing SLDS badge or pill blueprint already covers the needed visual pattern"), ("B", "Whether the org has a custom object with the same name"), ("C", "Whether using the standard component maintains consistency with native Lightning Experience"), ("D", "Whether a validation rule exists on the same object"), ("E", "Whether a roll-up summary field is present on the record")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 2], explanation: "Checking whether an existing SLDS blueprint already covers the needed pattern, and whether using it preserves visual consistency with native Lightning Experience, are the two considerations that directly determine whether a standard component is sufficient versus needing custom markup. B is incorrect because Having a custom object with the same name is unrelated to whether a visual badge pattern is appropriate. D is incorrect because A validation rule's presence has no bearing on the visual choice between a standard badge and custom markup. E is incorrect because A roll-up summary field's presence is unrelated to selecting a badge or pill visual pattern."),
        Question(id: "119", question: "A designer is reviewing a proposed color scheme for a custom Lightning component and wants to confirm it meets accessibility standards using an SLDS-aligned approach. Which action should they take?", options: [("A", "Use a color contrast checker to validate the proposed colors meet WCAG minimum contrast ratios before applying them via SLDS tokens"), ("B", "Skip contrast checking since SLDS tokens are automatically accessible by definition"), ("C", "Assume any SLDS-named token is compliant without verification"), ("D", "Only check contrast after the component is fully built and deployed to production")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Proactively validating proposed colors with a contrast checker before applying them ensures the chosen tokens will meet WCAG requirements, rather than assuming compliance or discovering issues post-deployment. B is incorrect because SLDS tokens provide consistency but don't automatically guarantee every custom combination meets contrast minimums; verification is still warranted. C is incorrect because Assuming compliance without verification risks shipping an inaccessible color combination. D is incorrect because Waiting until after production deployment to check contrast means accessibility issues reach real users before being caught."),
        Question(id: "120", question: "A developer wants to use an SLDS icon to represent the standard Account object in a custom navigation menu, matching how Account appears elsewhere in Lightning Experience. Which icon category should be used?", options: [("A", "Custom icon category"), ("B", "Standard icon category"), ("C", "Doctype icon category"), ("D", "Utility icon category")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "The standard icon category represents Salesforce's standard objects like Account, Contact, and Opportunity, ensuring visual consistency with how these objects appear elsewhere across Lightning Experience. A is incorrect because Custom icons are reserved for org-specific custom objects, not standard objects like Account. C is incorrect because Doctype icons represent file types, unrelated to representing a standard CRM object. D is incorrect because Utility icons represent generic interface actions and concepts, not specific object identity."),
        Question(id: "121", question: "A designer is evaluating whether a proposed custom component's spacing, colors, and iconography can be achieved entirely with existing SLDS resources versus requiring custom development. Which two SLDS resources should be checked first before writing any custom CSS? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "SLDS utility classes for spacing and layout needs"), ("B", "SLDS design tokens for color and typography values"), ("C", "A dependent picklist configuration"), ("D", "A roll-up summary field"), ("E", "A master-detail relationship")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Checking existing utility classes for spacing/layout and design tokens for color/typography values first ensures the designer exhausts built-in, consistency-preserving options before resorting to custom CSS. C is incorrect because A dependent picklist governs field value logic, entirely unrelated to visual styling resources. D is incorrect because A roll-up summary field aggregates data; it has no connection to visual styling decisions. E is incorrect because A master-detail relationship defines data structure, unrelated to evaluating available styling resources.")
    ]
}
